## Goal
Compute a Principal Component Analysis (PCA) of the RNA-seq samples using gene-level counts, and visualize the results as a scatter plot of the first two principal components with samples colored by experimental condition (untreated vs. treated).

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 00_data/rnaseq_bulk | samples.csv |

## Method
1. Load the gene count matrix (TSV) and sample metadata (CSV) using Python with pandas.
2. Filter genes with low expression (keep genes with ≥1 count in ≥2 samples) to reduce noise.
3. Apply log2-normalization to counts: log2(counts + 1) to stabilize variance.
4. Center and scale the log-normalized counts (z-score normalization) across genes.
5. Compute PCA using scikit-learn's PCA with all components; retain components explaining ≥95% cumulative variance.
6. Generate a scatter plot of PC1 vs. PC2 using matplotlib, with points colored by the `condition` column from samples.csv.
7. Add axis labels showing variance explained by each component, and a legend identifying conditions.
8. Save the figure as `results/pca_plot.png` (300 dpi).
Environment: gars-bio. All steps run as a single Python script in the analysis directory.

